follow one run end to end. reading gives what the code could do, every
branch live at once; a trace gives what it did.
`debugging/bisection:/where the bug could live/` is that sentence aimed
at a bug — this is it aimed at a system you do not know.

the program is an answer key that never runs out. `learning/predict`
needs something to check you, and everywhere else that is a book or a
teacher — scarce, coarse, late. code answers at any point, to any
depth, before you understand a line of it.

so: predict before you step. name where it goes and with what, then
step. stepping and watching is `learning/hindsight` at one frame per
step — every value obvious the moment it is on screen, nothing stored.

one trace is one path, and the happy path reads like the design. choose
the second to disagree with the first — the error, the empty input, the
call that hits the cache. traces that agree cost the same and teach
nothing.

pick the altitude: most steps in a deep stack are machinery, not the
idea. trace where the names are domain names, `general/naming/domain`.
it needs a start — `general/entrypoint` — and a run you can repeat,
`debugging/reproduce:/on demand/`, or you get one pass and no second
question.

keep it as addresses in order, `general/addressability`. the chain lives
in no single file, and every step either opens or fails loudly — which
is more than prose about a system can promise.
